<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">French Santo Domingo</h1><p class="st-meta">1795 CE – 1809 CE · capital Santo Domingo</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">French rule over the eastern, formerly Spanish part of Hispaniola, ceded in 1795, briefly held by Toussaint Louverture and ended by a Dominican revolt in 1809.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In the Treaty of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/canton-of-basel">Basel</a> of 1795 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> ceded its colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santo-domingo">Santo Domingo</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. France lacked the forces to take possession until January 1801, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toussaint-louverture">Toussaint Louverture</a> marched in, unified the island and abolished <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon's</a> expedition under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-leclerc-general">Charles Leclerc</a> took over in 1802, and after Haitian independence French troops held on in the east.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-louis-ferrand">Jean-Louis Ferrand</a> governed from 1804, reviving trade, restoring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> and encouraging raids on Haitian territory. He repulsed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-dessalines">Jean-Jacques Dessalines</a>, whose army besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santo-domingo">Santo Domingo</a> in 1805 and devastated <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/spanish-jamaica">Santiago</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moca-dominican-republic">Moca</a> on its retreat. The colony was poor and depopulated, as many Spanish-speaking families had emigrated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/venezuela">Venezuela</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1808, Dominican <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">creoles</a> led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-sanchez-ramirez">Juan Sánchez Ramírez</a> rose in revolt and defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-louis-ferrand">Ferrand</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-palo-hincado">Palo Hincado</a> in November 1808; Ferrand took his own life. With British naval help the rebels besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santo-domingo">Santo Domingo</a>, which surrendered in July 1809, restoring Spanish rule in the period called <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/espana-boba">España Boba</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="colony, 1795 CE – 1809 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 64 H80 L70 78 H30 Z"/><path d="M50 64 V18"/><path d="M50 22 L72 56 H50"/><path d="M50 28 L32 56 H50"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">colony</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:95.42%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1795 CE – 1809 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Santo Domingo</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Toussaint Louverture, Jean-Louis Ferrand</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish and French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/dominican-republic">Dominican Republic</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Santo Domingo (French)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1802&amp;focus=french-santo-domingo" aria-label="French Santo Domingo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2785 1493 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where French Santo Domingo